 "causes": {
  "INT32-WRAP": "JS wraps every Int operation to int32 (`|0`); this backend runs wider integer arithmetic, so the JS values are the OVERFLOWED ones. `topInt`/`bottomInt` are foreign imports copied from JS while the arithmetic underneath is wider; Gate C9 / review X8 settles it.",
  "REM-ZERO": "`Int.rem x 0` is NaN on JS, a value that is not an Int at all. No backend with a real integer type can reproduce it; this one answers 0.",
  "ASTRAL": "JS counts UTF-16 code units; this backend counts codepoints. Identical for BMP text.",
  "STACK": "JS captures a stack when an Error is constructed; this runtime has one only once an exception is raised (or, on Go, while panicking). `Maybe` is the honest type.",
  "SURROGATE": "A Go string is UTF-8 and cannot hold a lone surrogate, so one round-trips as U+FFFD. Structural, not a shim choice."
 },
